<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Information Age</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Information Age is a historical period that began in the mid-20th century, marked by a rapid shift from the traditional industries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> to an economy centred on information technology. Its onset has been linked to the first working <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistor</a>, invented in 1947 by John Bardeen and Walter Houser Brattain under William Shockley at Bell Labs, which led the way to more compact digital computers. The digital revolution converted technology from analog to digital format, so that identical copies could be made; a message was sent over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpanet">ARPANET</a> in 1969, and in the 1980s compact discs replaced vinyl records and cassette tapes.</p></div>
